  "answer_text": "The term **\"iCloud control\"** can refer to several different utilities and settings depending on the operating system and device you are using. \n\n---\n\n### 1. iCloud for Windows (Formerly \"iCloud Control Panel\")\nIf you are using a Windows PC, \"iCloud Control Panel\" is the legacy name for **iCloud for Windows**. This official app from Apple allows you to sync and manage your iCloud data directly on your PC.\n* **Features:**\n  * **iCloud Drive:** Access and organize files from File Explorer.\n  * **iCloud Photos:** Sync your photos directly into the Microsoft Photos app.\n  * **iCloud Passwords:** Access your iCloud Keychain and autofill passwords in Chrome, Edge, or Firefox.\n  * **Mail, Contacts, and Calendars:** Sync your Outlook calendar and contacts.\n* **How to get it:** You can download it for free directly from the **Microsoft Store** or via Apple\u2019s support website.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Native Offline File Controls (iOS 18+ & macOS Sequoia+)\nFor a long time, users complained that Apple's automated system would randomly \"offload\" local files to the cloud to save space, leaving them unable to open files when offline. \n\nApple officially resolved this by introducing native **Keep Downloaded** controls:\n* **How to use on iPhone & iPad (iOS 18+):** Open the **Files** app, navigate to iCloud Drive, long-press a file or folder, and select **Keep Downloaded**.\n* **How to use on Mac (macOS Sequoia/15+):** Open **Finder**, click on iCloud Drive in the sidebar, right-click (or Control-click) a file or folder, and select **Keep Downloaded**.\n* This forces iCloud to keep a local, offline-accessible copy on your hard drive permanently.\n\n---\n\n### 3. \"iCloud-Control\" (Third-Party macOS Utility)\nIf you are on an older version of macOS (or prefer a dedicated interface), **iCloud-Control** is a popular, free, open-source utility available on GitHub. \n* **What it does:** It adds a menu to the macOS Finder toolbar, giving you manual, selective synchronization over your iCloud Drive files\u2014allowing you to easily force-download or manually \"evict\" (remove local copy while keeping on the cloud) specific files and folders.\n* **How to install:** If you use Homebrew, you can install it using the following terminal command:\n  ```bash\n  brew install --cask icloud-control\n  ```\n\n---\n\n### 4. General iCloud Control Settings\nIf you simply want to access your standard Apple account and storage configurations, you can find them here:\n* **On iPhone/iPad:** Go to **Settings** > **[Your Name]** > **iCloud**.\n* **On Mac:** Go to **Apple Menu (\uf8ff)** > **System Settings** > **[Your Name]** (at the top) > **iCloud**.\n* **On the Web:** Log in to **iCloud.com** from any web browser to view, delete, or recover your files, photos, notes, and emails.",
